<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Vyhovsky</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1664</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ivan Vyhovskyi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Vyhovsky was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a> of the Zaporozhian Host from 1657 to 1659, after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>. Seeking to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> from Muscovite control, he concluded the Treaty of Hadiach with Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1658 and, with Crimean allies, defeated a Russian army at Konotop in 1659. Opposition among the Cossacks forced him to resign, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> executed him in 1664.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-vyhovsky.jpg" alt="Ivan Vyhovsky" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Havrylo Vasko / After Samiilo Vasyliovych Velychko · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_Vyhovsky_(Portrait,_19th_century,_after_restoration).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
